WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:19.040
NHS Operational Planning and Contracting Guidance for 2017–2019

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.040
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Simon has stolen part
of my thunder, so thank you for that, Chief

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:30.860
Executive. As Simon said, the operational
planning guidance is now out; it went out

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:37.780
on 22nd September. I think generally a positive
reception from the service, and thanks to

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:44.730
NHSI, with whom we developed the work jointly,
and to our other partners who input into that

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:50.329
piece of work. I think the theme that we were
looking to get into the planning guidance

00:00:50.329 --> 00:00:57.200
was to create stability and certainty going
forwards, and to create an environment where

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.000
health systems could plan together, building
on the work they have been doing on the STPs,

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:07.850
and to avoid the risk that these became two
separate exercises: the contracting process

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:13.779
and the STP process.
To this end, we brought the process forward

00:01:13.779 --> 00:01:18.579
by three months in order that we can get it
done by the end of this year and, going into

00:01:18.579 --> 00:01:24.420
next year, the NHS can focus on the doing
rather than on the planning. We have published

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:30.659
for consultation the two-year tariff and the
standard contract. We have published two-year

00:01:30.659 --> 00:01:36.479
CQUINs and CCG quality premiums, so that there
is clarity around that, and we have kept the

00:01:36.479 --> 00:01:44.310
nine must-dos, the quality metrics, the same,
so we have not dropped a whole load of new

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:51.189
targets into the system. To reduce the bureaucracy
and the overhead, we have brought NHS England

00:01:51.189 --> 00:01:56.189
and NHSI together into a single oversight
process.

00:01:56.189 --> 00:02:01.819
Bringing forward the contracting timetable
to the end of this year has allowed us to

00:02:01.819 --> 00:02:07.009
align it with the STPs, which I think was
crucial because, most importantly, what we

00:02:07.009 --> 00:02:12.060
have been trying to do is to create an environment
whereby the joint planning that has gone on

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:19.769
in every health system over the four- to five-year
future is now reflected in the actual plans

00:02:19.769 --> 00:02:26.319
that are written for the next two years. In
order to enable that, we have created control

00:02:26.319 --> 00:02:31.370
totals for health systems, so that organisations
can work together to say how the money is

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:36.620
best deployed across their health system in
order to optimise the health output.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:41.709
It has been a big piece of work, and thanks
to all the people who have worked on it. The

00:02:41.709 --> 00:02:52.450
NHS is now deeply engaged in taking this forward,
and I think it sets a new direction for joint

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:58.800
planning, long-term strategy going forward,
as an approach to delivery, which I think

00:02:58.800 --> 00:02:59.960
is overall very positive.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:06.020
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Matthew. May I
invite the Board to offer observations or

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:13.180
queries? Matthew, what could possibly go wrong?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Obviously, nothing could

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:22.129
possibly go wrong! This is a big piece of
work. The NHS is going through a large set

00:03:22.129 --> 00:03:27.670
of changes. The Five Year Forward View has
set an extremely ambitious agenda for the

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:36.319
NHS. It is going to require us to rethink
the model of care to deliver services focused

00:03:36.319 --> 00:03:39.760
on keeping people well, focused on health
improvement, focused on keeping people out

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:46.320
of hospital, and that is going to require
us to move away from just doing more of the

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:52.740
same, into actually, what should health look
like for a particular population? We have

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:58.500
asked a hard question of the NHS, whilst it
is under pressure from growing demand and

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:05.800
whilst finances and capital are constrained,
but all of the signs coming out of the STP

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:12.720
work are that people are heads down, really
wanting to deliver, and I think that is positive.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:18.060
However, this is not a form-filling exercise;
this is a real piece of management and planning.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:26.760
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Just supplementing that:
in terms of things that are risk points, the

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:32.770
financial exit position for 2016/2017, both
for CCGs and for trusts, is obviously a substantial

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:36.970
thing we have to get right. Paul will talk
more about that in his report. If we do not

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:42.450
effectively deliver the end-year financial
position through trusts and CCGs that we need

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:46.490
to, then 2017/2018 is going to be very, very
difficult.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:52.699
Secondly, NHS Improvement and NHS England
will shortly be issuing proposed 2017/2018,

00:04:52.699 --> 00:05:01.340
2018/2019 control totals for individual organisations,
so not too much process of faffing around

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:06.419
on that rather than actually those getting
those understood, accepted and then people

00:05:06.419 --> 00:05:13.119
working collectively to set themselves right
for next year. I think that could otherwise

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:16.440
come at a big opportunity cost to management
focus.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:25.020
Thirdly, we signalled quite explicitly that
people are free to move away from activity-based

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:30.419
payments in their local health economy where
the relevant organisations can agree. That

00:05:30.419 --> 00:05:34.550
is not to say they have to, but if they want
to, they should not regard that as an obstacle

00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:43.449
or think of us standing in their way. The
reality is that that requires a new set of

00:05:43.449 --> 00:05:49.090
ways of constructing relationships and dialogue
about what the future should look like. Given

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:54.599
how tight funding is going to be next year
and the year after, people are not going to

00:05:54.599 --> 00:06:01.360
be able to just trade their way out of problems
with ramped volumes. Equally, CCGs are not

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:05.159
just going to be able to pretend there are
not demand pressures when there are. So we

00:06:05.159 --> 00:06:09.750
are just trying to get people to focus on
the honest constraints and conversations that

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:13.930
are required, rather than retreating to their
corners and trying to slug it out.

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:23.380
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: I was just wondering,
looking across the 44 STPs, where the biggest

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:28.900
risks lie. Obviously, Simon has already explained
that they are in different stages of development,

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:33.340
which is to be understood. However, looking
across the piece, and given the time constraints

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:38.470
that have been placed on the planning of the
STP process, where do you land the biggest

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:44.530
risks? Is it the public/patient engagement
piece, is it the reality of financial planning?

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:52.260
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: I would say that there
are two things that the STPs really need to

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:58.349
get right. One of them is very much reflecting
what Simon has said, that this is a planning

00:06:58.349 --> 00:07:04.819
in partnership exercise, so people are not
going back into their silos but actually working

00:07:04.819 --> 00:07:11.729
together to say, across health and social
care, across primary and secondary care, how

00:07:11.729 --> 00:07:17.800
do we use the money most effectively rather
than going back into, how do I protect my

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:23.150
organisation? We have seen that coming together
with the STPs, and I think the biggest risk

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:28.220
is that people retreat back into a traditional
contracting process, so that is one big risk.

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:36.360
I think the other area that STPs absolutely
have to get right is the community engagement,

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:42.160
the patient engagement, the public engagement
in the set of changes that will emerge from

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:45.930
this that we are going to need to discuss
and engage with people who are using the services.

00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:53.139
Because we are expecting STPs to be able to
present options for how services will get

00:07:53.139 --> 00:07:59.500
better as well as how they get more efficient,
and getting that story across and building

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:06.719
a public understanding and confidence in this
I think is going to require an openness and

00:08:06.719 --> 00:08:09.759
a dialogue that we need to make sure we get
right.

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:14.840
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much. I think
the Board has seen over the last few years

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:19.490
a steady improvement in the production of
the planning guidance. I still recall a Christmas

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:27.009
Eve of a rushed-through document that nobody
had ever properly read, then panic through

00:08:27.009 --> 00:08:33.070
to March, then no finalisation even by then.
So to be able to get the guidance out now,

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:39.630
the threat of arbitration oversight by two
distinguished chief executives and, actually,

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:44.860
the expectation of getting to agreement and,
as Simon put it, getting away from the deflection

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:48.400
of senior management time and the opportunity
costs, and getting on with the business, I

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:54.840
think is a major step forward. So I am sure
the Board welcomes the new guidance and we

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:59.410
look forward to following its progress through.
Control totals will be another part of the

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:06.960
structure, but I do not think that we could
at this stage anticipate any better approach

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:08.860
to what are trying to do with the STPs.

